# Spec — Bidirectional bridges (GitHub/GitLab)

Roadmap step 6/6 (parent 0e7b12). Depends on step 4 (op-log). High effort, DEFERRED.

## Goal

Sync tickets bidirectionally with external trackers (GitHub Issues, GitLab, …),
mirroring git-bug's bridge pattern, so teams straddling ticgit and a hosted
tracker keep a single source of truth in git.

## Current state

- ticgit already has one-directional import: `ti import gh|linear` (see
  `reference.md`). This step generalizes to continuous, bidirectional sync.
- The op-log (step 4) is the enabler: external changes map to ops, so merges with
  local edits are conflict-free and re-import is idempotent.

## Design

- Bridge config per remote tracker: target platform, URL, auth token, mapping
  rules. Follow git-bug's `bridge new --target=<platform> --url=... --token=...`
  shape for familiarity. Store config locally (tokens NEVER in synced git-meta).
- Import direction: external issue/comment → ticgit op (reuse step 4 op kinds).
  Keep a stable external-id ↔ ticket-uuid mapping (in `meta:` fields or a bridge
  namespace) so repeated syncs dedupe instead of duplicating.
- Export direction: local ops → external API calls, with a watermark (last-synced
  op head) per bridge so only new ops are pushed.
- Conflict handling: because the op-log is the truth, a round-trip is a merge of
  op sets, not a destructive overwrite; field-level divergence resolves by the
  same (lamport, hash) rule as local merges.

## Changes

- New `bridge` module + `ti bridge` command family (new/list/pull/push/configure).
- Per-platform adapter trait; implement GitHub first, GitLab second.
- Auth/token storage local-only; rate-limit + pagination handling per platform.

## Acceptance (when undeferred)

- Round-trip test against a recorded/mock GitHub API: external issue imports as a
  ticket; local edit exports back; re-sync is idempotent (no duplicates, no
  clobber).

## Why deferred

- Highest effort, external-API surface, and not required for the core distributed-
  tracker story. Scheduled last; revisit after steps 1–5 land and the op-log has
  proven stable in real use.